    {
      "type": "agent_message",
      "text": "I’m using the carrylog-continuity skill because this is a portable-resume verification task. Its `SKILL.md` lies under a policy-denied path, so I can’t read or apply it directly; I’ll follow the repository’s checked-in context instructions and restrict verification to the three registered local checks."
    },
    {
      "type": "agent_message",
      "text": "The reviewed envelope is from a later pilot checkpoint, so I won’t treat its clean status as proof about `c9e8c2d`. I’m now running the three frozen checks; the continuity check is the executable evidence for resource and freshness boundaries, while the diff check confirms this verification made no changes."
    },
    {
      "type": "agent_message",
      "text": "Verified the portable-resume contract at `c9e8c2d`:\n\n- Per file: **1 MiB** (`1,048,576` bytes) for managed configuration and each canonical context document.\n- Aggregate: **8 MiB** (`8,388,608` bytes) across canonical-context reads for one resume observation.\n- Always loaded: **16,000 JavaScript characters** total by default—JavaScript string units, not bytes or tokenizer tokens.\n\nStructural validation and freshness are separate:\n\n- `carrylog validate` verifies schema, required document structure, and managed surfaces.\n- It does not prove checkpoint Git freshness or semantic freshness.\n- Resume reports stale checkpoint evidence; strict `resume --check` fails when that evidence is stale even if structural validation succeeds.\n\nLocal checks run:\n\n- `format:check`: passed, 65 files checked.\n- Focused continuity tests: passed, 16/16.\n- Git diff check: passed with exit 0.\n\nNo files were modified, staged, or committed."
    },
